/* GENERATED by scripts/legal/privacy.mjs from docs/privacy-policy.md. Do not edit: edit the Markdown file and run `npm run privacy`. */
const PRIVACY_MD = "# OpenTick Privacy Policy\n\n**Last updated:** 25 November 2026\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nOpenTick is a free, open-source task management and productivity application developed by JebbyCodes. This privacy policy explains how OpenTick handles information when you use the application.\n\nOpenTick is designed to work locally on your device without requiring an account, subscription, or developer-operated cloud service.\n\n## 2. Information collected by OpenTick\n\nOpenTick is designed not to collect or transmit your personal information to the developer. It has no analytics, advertising or crash reporting, and it sends no usage data unless you turn something on. The one thing that resembles telemetry is an optional, anonymous count of which pack-library packs get used. It is **off until you turn it on** and is described under \"Pack usage counts\" in section 8.\n\nInformation you enter into the application, such as tasks, notes, templates, countdowns, saved time zones, boards, projects, timetables, holidays, habits, attachments, contacts (the names, email addresses, phone numbers, available hours and other details of people you choose to add), the sections you create yourself and their entries, and other productivity data, is stored locally on your device.\n\nOpenTick does not require you to provide a name, email address, or other account details to use its core functionality.\n\n## 3. Local storage and control of your data\n\nYour locally stored application data remains under your control. You can export it, back it up, or delete it from Settings → Data & sync, and, where applicable, through the storage facilities provided by your operating system.\n\nUninstalling OpenTick may not remove every file containing application data. Back up any important information before uninstalling or resetting the application.\n\nYour preferences (theme, quiet hours, accessibility options, clock format, keyboard hints and similar choices) are part of your data: if you turn on sync they travel with everything else, sealed with your passphrase when encryption is on. Settings that describe one device (window layout and sizes, pinned views, background services, the update-check choice, the system bridge, sounds) are stored on that device only and are never synced. If you choose your own reminder sound, the audio file is kept on the device only, like an attachment: it is never synced, never sent anywhere, and is removed when you choose the built-in sound again or reset the settings.\n\n## 4. Optional sync and backups\n\nSync is off by default. If you turn it on, OpenTick copies your data to a location **you** choose and control (for example a WebDAV server, a Git repository, an S3-compatible bucket, or a file in a folder). The developer does not operate this service and cannot see your data. The provider you pick has its own privacy policy, and whoever runs that server can read what is stored there unless you turn on encryption.\n\nSync credentials (such as a password or token) are kept on your device only and are not included in the synced data.\n\n## 5. Encryption\n\nYou can protect your data with a passphrase in Settings → Encryption. When enabled, the data stored on this device, in automatic backups, and in sync files is encrypted with AES-256-GCM using a key derived from your passphrase. Without the passphrase (or the recovery key you were shown) the data cannot be read, and the developer cannot recover it for you.\n\nAttachments and a custom reminder sound are stored in the device's own database and are encrypted with the same key when encryption is on. Countdowns, saved time zones and board headings are part of the same data: they are stored, backed up and synced like your tasks, and are encrypted with them when encryption is on.\n\n**Contacts, your own sections and packs.** Contacts hold information about other people that you type in yourself (and the teachers you add to Classes appear there too). They, the sections you create and their entries are part of your data in every way described above: stored on your device, included in backups and exports, synced only to the place you choose, and encrypted when encryption is on. Opening a person's email address or phone number hands it to your device's mail or phone app; OpenTick sends nothing itself. A *pack* is a small file of sections and colours that you can export, add from a file or pasted text, or get from the pack library (see section 8). A pack contains no code. By default it cannot read or change anything you already have; a pack that asks for a permission (for example to make a task from an entry) says so before it is added, and you choose what to allow and can change that later. Your decisions, a list of what each pack did or was refused (it names the permission involved but never holds the contents of a task, note or reminder) and the reminders a pack declares are kept on your device and in your backups and sync like everything else, encrypted when encryption is on, and are never sent to the pack library or to the developer. Exporting one never includes the people linked to your entries, and adding one from a file or pasted text makes no network request. Please add other people's details only where you have a reason to keep them, and remove them if they ask.\n\nWhen encryption is on, home-screen widgets, the tray menu and background notifications show only generic text, unless you choose to allow a readable summary for them.\n\n## 6. Notifications and the background service\n\nOpenTick can show reminders for tasks, lessons and timetable slots, and an optional daily summary. These are created on your device from your own data.\n\nTo make sure reminders are not missed, OpenTick can run in the background: on desktop it can stay in the system tray, start when you sign in, and notify while the window is closed; on Android it can schedule alarms, restart them after the device reboots, and show an optional persistent notification with your current and next lesson. All of this happens on your device. Nothing is sent to the developer or to any server. You can turn each part off in Settings → Notifications.\n\n## 7. Permissions\n\nDepending on the platform, OpenTick may ask for:\n\n- **Notifications** – to show reminders and the optional persistent timetable notification.\n- **Run at start-up / receive boot completed (Android), launch at login (desktop)** – to restore reminders and the background service after a restart.\n- **Exact alarms (Android)** – so reminders arrive on time.\n- **Foreground service (Android)** – only for the optional persistent timetable notification.\n- **File access** – only for files you choose to attach, import or export.\n\nOpenTick does not request access to your contacts, location, camera, microphone or accounts. Time zones are read from your device's own settings; your location is never used.\n\n**Connecting other apps (optional, off by default).** Settings → Integrations lets you allow other apps on your device to read or add tasks. Nothing is shared until you approve each app and choose what it may do; you can disconnect it at any time. Pairing records and their tokens stay on this device and are never part of sync files or backups. OpenTick sends nothing to any server for this: apps talk to OpenTick through links and messages on your own device, and replies go only to the address the other app supplied.\n\n## 8. Network connections and third-party services\n\nOpenTick makes network requests only when you ask it to: for example to sync with the provider you configured, to load an embedded video, audio or web player that you put in a note, or to check for a newer version (see below). Those services are governed by their own privacy policies.\n\n**Update check.** It is off by default. If you turn on Settings → About & privacy → \"Check for updates automatically\", OpenTick asks GitHub's public release information for this project (api.github.com) whether a newer release exists, at most once a day while the app is open. \"Check now\" sends one such request, only when you press it. The request contains nothing about you, your data or your settings; as with any web request, GitHub can see the IP address it comes from and a generic client name, and handles that under its own privacy policy. OpenTick uses the answer only to show the version number and a link to the release page, and never downloads or installs anything by itself.\n\n**Pack library.** Settings → Sections & packs → Pack library, \"Add a pack by code\" and the command palette entries ask the pack library (the project's OpenTick-Packs website on GitHub Pages, jebbycodes.github.io) for its list of packs, and for the one pack you pick, only when you press the button or type a code. Nothing is requested in the background or when you open Settings (apart from the optional usage count described next, which is off unless you turn it on). The requests contain nothing about you, your data or your settings, and no account or identifier is used; as with any web request, GitHub can see the IP address it comes from and a generic client name, and handles that under its own privacy policy. What comes back is data (a list, and a pack of sections and colours): it is checked before it is shown, you see what is in a pack before anything is added, and what you add is stored like the rest of your data. A link such as `?pack=CODE` on the web app opens the \"Add a pack by code\" dialog for that code and asks the library only after the app has opened.\n\n**Pack usage counts (optional, off by default).** Settings → Sections & packs → Pack usage counts lets you help the pack library's website show how many times each pack is used. It is off unless you turn it on, and while it is off OpenTick makes no request and makes no random code. (If you have turned it on before, your device keeps a short list of which packs it already counted, only on your device, so that turning it back on does not count them twice.) If you turn it on, OpenTick asks the library once for its public list, to learn the address of the usage service. After that, the first time you really use a pack that you added *from the library* on this device (saving or ticking off one of its entries, running one of its actions, or choosing one of its colour themes), OpenTick sends that service the pack's id and a random code made for that pack on this device, and nothing else: no entries, notes, tasks, names or field values, no account, user or device identifier, and no OpenTick version. The code exists only so that a retry is not counted twice; it is deleted from your device as soon as the count is confirmed, is never used for anything else and is not synced. The service, which the project's maintainer runs on Cloudflare, keeps a total per pack and a hash of each code for about two months (so a repeat is not counted twice); it does not store your IP address, which, like any web request, the request carries, and Cloudflare handles it under its own privacy policy. Adding a pack, browsing the library and downloading a file are never counted, and a pack from a file or pasted text is never counted. If the service cannot be reached, the count waits on your device and is tried again later, which can happen in the background but only while the setting is on. Turning it off discards any count that is still waiting. Counts are per device: your other devices count separately, and only if you turn the setting on there. What the service keeps and how it works is documented in the library's `usage/README.md`.\n\nThird-party services that you access independently, including GitHub when viewing the project's website or reporting an issue, are governed by their own privacy policies.\n\nIf future versions introduce optional online features, integrations, or telemetry, this policy will be updated to describe the relevant data handling.\n\n## 9. Data sharing and sale\n\nThe developer does not sell personal information collected through OpenTick. OpenTick is not intended to transmit the contents of your tasks, notes, templates, or projects to the developer.\n\n## 10. Children's privacy\n\nOpenTick is a general-purpose productivity application. The developer does not intentionally collect personal information from children through the application.\n\n## 11. Security\n\nKeeping your device secure and maintaining backups helps protect your locally stored information. You are responsible for the security of your device, your passphrase and any backups you create.\n\n## 12. Changes to this policy\n\nThis privacy policy may be updated when OpenTick's features or data practices change. The latest version is published with the project and is shown inside the app (Settings → About & privacy).\n\n## 13. Contact\n\nFor questions about this privacy policy or OpenTick's privacy practices, contact the developer through the project's GitHub repository:\n\nhttps://github.com/JebbyCodes/OpenTick\n";
